      "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{d+1}{\\sqrt{2d}+1}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{2d}-1.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{2d}-1,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d+1}{\\sqrt{2d}+1} & = \\\\ \\frac{d+1}{\\sqrt{2d}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2d}-1}{\\sqrt{2d}-1} & = \\\\ \\frac{(d+1)(\\sqrt{2d}-1)}{(\\sqrt{2d}+1)(\\sqrt{2d}-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{(d+1)(\\sqrt{2d}-1)}{(\\sqrt{2d})^{2}-{1}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{(d+1)(\\sqrt{2d}-1)}{2d-1} & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
